\begin{proof}
Let \{W_i\}_{i\in I} be any collection of subspaces of the vector space $V$ over the field $F$, and set $W=\bigcap_{i\in I}W_i$. If $I$ is empty, then by convention $W=V$, which is a subspace. Assume otherwise. Since every subspace contains $0$, we have $0\in W_i$ for every $i\in I$, and hence $0\in W$. If $u,v\in W$, then $u,v\in W_i$ for every $i\in I$. Because each $W_i$ is closed under addition, $u+v\in W_i$ for every $i\in I$, so $u+v\in W$. Likewise, if $a\in F$ and $v\in W$, then $v\in W_i$ for every $i\in I$; scalar closure of each $W_i$ gives $av\in W_i$ for every $i\in I$, hence $av\in W$. Therefore $W$ is a subspace of $V$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $\{V_i\}_{i\in I}$ be any collection of subspaces of $V$, and let $S=\bigcap_{i\in I}V_i$. By definition, $S\subseteq V$. If the collection is empty, then $S=V$, so the conclusion is immediate. Otherwise, each $V_i$ contains the zero vector, and therefore $0\in S$. For $x,y\in S$, we have $x,y\in V_i$ for every $i\in I$. Since every $V_i$ is a subspace, $x+y\in V_i$ for every $i\in I$, and thus $x+y\in S$. Finally, for any scalar $a\in F$ and any $x\in S$, scalar closure of every $V_i$ implies $ax\in V_i$ for every $i\in I$, so $ax\in S$. Hence $S$ is a subspace of $V$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $\{V_i\}_{i\in I}$ be any collection of subspaces of $V$ and put $S=\bigcap_{i\in I}V_i$. We have $S\subseteq V$. Under the standard convention, if $I=\varnothing$ then $S=V$, so $S$ is a subspace. Assume $I\ne\varnothing$. For every $i\in I$, the subspace $V_i$ contains $0$, hence $0\in S$. Let $x,y\in S$. Then $x,y\in V_i$ for each $i\in I$. Since $V_i$ is a subspace, $x+y\in V_i$ for each $i\in I$, and consequently $x+y\in S$. If $a\in F$ and $x\in S$, then $x\in V_i$ for each $i\in I$, so $ax\in V_i$ for each $i\in I$ by scalar closure. Therefore $ax\in S$. Thus $S$ contains $0$ and is closed under addition and scalar multiplication, so $S$ is a subspace of $V$.
\end{proof}
